INSERT INTO "BlogPost" ("id","title","slug","excerpt","content","coverImage","coverImageAlt","readTime","authorId","categoryId","published","featured","publishedAt","editorialUpdatedAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('blog-ai-search-20261020','AI Search for Small Businesses: What Actually Matters','ai-search-for-small-businesses','Before paying for AI search optimisation, audit three website pages. Check your service details, project evidence and enquiry journey with this practical guide.','<p>Before you pay for an AI search package, open your website and ask a less fashionable question: could a stranger work out exactly what you do, who you help and why they should trust you?</p>
<p>If the answer takes a sales call, your first investment may belong on the website itself.</p>
<p>For a small business, a sensible approach to AI search starts with accessible pages, clear service information and evidence behind your claims. Then measure whether the visibility brings suitable enquiries. A new acronym does not remove that work.</p>
<h2>Why this question matters now</h2>
<p>Google''s <a href="https://blog.google/products-and-platforms/products/search/search-io-2026/">May 2026 Search announcement</a> put AI-powered search and agents at the centre of its product direction. Business owners are having to decide where these changes belong in their marketing budgets.</p>
<p>You will see the terms generative engine optimisation (GEO) and answer engine optimisation (AEO). Google''s <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/ai-optimization-guide">current guidance for its generative search features</a> says SEO remains relevant. It does not require a special AI writing style, an llms.txt file or special AI markup. Eligibility does not guarantee inclusion.</p>
<p>That guidance concerns Google Search. Other AI services have their own access rules and discovery systems; a claim about Google should not be presented as a rule for every assistant.</p>
<p>So ask a prospective supplier what they will actually change. “AI visibility” could mean useful technical and editorial work. It could also conceal a report full of screenshots with no plan for improving the business underneath.</p>
<h2>Start with three pages and a buyer''s questions</h2>
<p>Here is an audit you can do without buying another tool. Choose your homepage, the page describing your main service and one completed project or case study. Where your services share one page, review the relevant section.</p>
<p>Use the table below. For every answer, record the page URL and the exact sentence or piece of evidence a visitor can find. “We explain this on calls” means the website still has a gap.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Page</th><th>Question to answer</th><th>Evidence to look for</th><th>Your next edit</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Homepage</td><td>Is this business relevant to me?</td><td>A specific service, intended customer and clear next step</td><td>Replace a vague opening with an offer people can understand</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main service</td><td>What would I receive?</td><td>Deliverables, boundaries, working process and information needed for a quote</td><td>Answer the buying question customers repeatedly ask</td></tr>
<tr><td>Completed project</td><td>Can this team show relevant work?</td><td>An accurate brief, delivered work and a live example where permission allows</td><td>Add verifiable detail; remove unsupported results</td></tr>
<tr><td>All three</td><td>How do I make contact?</td><td>A working enquiry route and consistent business details</td><td>Test the journey on a phone and assign responsibility for replies</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>This is an editorial review tool, not a Google score or a prediction of AI citations. Its purpose is to reveal missing information before you spend money attracting more visitors.</p>
<p>For example, imagine a consultant offering website development to overseas clients. “We build stunning digital experiences” leaves a buyer guessing. A useful service description would explain the agreed pages or features, who supplies content, how revisions work, which accounts the client owns and what support follows launch. This is a hypothetical example, not a client result.</p>
<p>Those details also give you better material for articles. Use our <a href="https://www.entrehubng.com/blog/content-marketing-creating-value-for-your-audience">content brief for real customer questions</a> to turn one unanswered question into a focused piece.</p>
<h2>Make the evidence easy to inspect</h2>
<p>A portfolio entry needs to distinguish the client''s business from the work you delivered. If you developed the website, say so. Do not imply that you created its branding, wrote its content or produced its sales unless those statements are true.</p>
<p>A modest completed project with a clear scope is more useful to a buyer than an impressive percentage with no explanation. Where results are available and approved for publication, state the period, what was measured and the limits of that measurement. Where they are unavailable, describe the delivery accurately.</p>
<p>Our <a href="https://www.entrehubng.com/#work">completed project portfolio</a> gives readers a place to inspect examples of our website work. The same principle applies to a training business, a designer or a professional practice: show the relevant evidence you have permission to share.</p>
<h2>Check the route from search to an enquiry</h2>
<p>Ask whoever manages your website to check whether important pages can be crawled and indexed, whether their preferred URLs are consistent and whether visitors can read the main content on a phone. Begin with our <a href="https://www.entrehubng.com/blog/seo-best-practices-for-small-businesses">small-business SEO checklist</a> if you need to prioritise those checks.</p>
<p>Use descriptive internal links to connect related pages. Google''s <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/seo-starter-guide">SEO Starter Guide</a> explains how links support discovery and help readers understand where they are going. “Website handover checklist” tells someone more than “click here”.</p>
<p>Then test the actual enquiry route. Submit a legitimate test message, confirm that it arrives and identify who replies. Keep test enquiries separate from customer enquiries in your records. A visible site with a broken contact journey still loses opportunities.</p>
<h2>Give other people a useful reason to link</h2>
<p>Publish something another business owner can use: a clear comparison, a checklist they can copy or a properly documented project. This article''s three-page audit is intended to be used that way.</p>
<p>Share the useful part on your business networks and link to the full resource for context. When approaching a relevant community or publication, explain how the resource helps its readers. Let the editor decide whether to include it and how to attribute it.</p>
<p>Google''s <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies#link-spam">link-spam policy</a> covers tactics such as paid links for ranking purposes, excessive link exchanges and automated link creation. A social post can bring readers to your site; it does not establish that you have earned an independent editorial backlink or ranking improvement.</p>
<h2>What should a paid proposal contain?</h2>
<p>Ask for the pages being improved, the problem on each page, the proposed edit and the way progress will be assessed. For a technical task, request a before-and-after check. For content, request the buying question it answers and the evidence it will use.</p>
<p>Keep a dated record of published changes. Review search visibility alongside engaged visits, suitable enquiries and completed sales, using consistent definitions. Our <a href="https://www.entrehubng.com/blog/analytics-and-data-driven-marketing-decisions">enquiry-tracking worksheet</a> can help connect activity with outcomes. A screenshot showing one AI answer is a useful observation, but it cannot establish a reliable pipeline of customers.</p>
<p>If the three-page audit exposes gaps in your own website, <a href="https://www.entrehubng.com/#contact">tell Entrehub Services what you offer and who you want to reach</a>. We can discuss the website, content or SEO work needed to make the next buying decision easier.</p>
